# UX and Visual System

## Experience character

The product should feel cinematic but restrained, natural, quietly futuristic, premium, human, scholarly, tactile, field-notebook inspired, and highly legible outdoors.

Avoid generic SaaS dashboard aesthetics, neon science-fiction styling, childish educational visuals, constant progress bars, excessive gradients or glassmorphism, dense field overlays, and gamification that competes with the place.

## Mode-specific design

### Learner Mode

- mobile-first
- image-led
- one primary task per screen
- large type and touch targets
- minimal chrome
- explicit capture choices
- hints hidden until requested
- no automatic lecture panels
- field mode quieter than creator mode

### Creator Mode

- desktop/tablet first
- map + structured editing
- learning architecture made visible
- contextual preview
- safety, accessibility, evidence, adaptation, and resurfacing are first-class fields

### Orchestrator Mode

- learner cards and recent evidence
- recommendations editable and overridable
- limited meaningful alerts
- no vanity analytics

### Reviewer Mode

- serious checklist
- unresolved issues visible
- current approval status unambiguous
- no false implication of legal, park, or youth-program approval

## Design tokens

Conceptual palette:

- tidal navy
- cypress green
- marsh olive
- parchment
- water blue
- amber for questions
- muted violet for mentor adaptations
- soft red for safety only

Use CSS variables. Do not hardcode colors throughout components.

## Accessibility

- 44px minimum touch targets
- visible keyboard focus
- reduced-motion support
- semantic landmarks
- alt text
- no color-only communication
- accessible list alternative for maps
- transcripts/captions for audio
